#!/usr/bin/env node
/** Apply editorial translations for siman 132 part 1/3 (40 blocks). */
import fs from "fs";
import path from "path";
import { fileURLToPath } from "url";

const ROOT = path.resolve(path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), "..");
const OUT = path.join(ROOT, "output", "siman_132");

/** @type {Record<string, string>} key = relative path#slug#seif#marker */
const T = {
  "ateret-zekenim/part-001.txt#ateret-zekenim#1#_": `And afterward the chazzan recites half-kaddish on the verses of the station, in order to make a separation between them and the verses that they will say afterward; then they say Ashrei and Lamnatzeiach and the order of kedushah, and afterward one kaddish for all of them.`,

  "ateret-zekenim/part-001.txt#ateret-zekenim#2#_": `After the conclusion of the prayer they say Aleinu, and there are those who wrote that one should say Aleinu after every prayer, even after Minchah (Lechem HaShamayim). And one should say "and the seat of His glory is in the heavens" and not say "and the throne of His glory" (Tur in siman 133 and responsa of Maharshal siman 68). And they say the song that the Levites would say in the Temple only at Shacharit, and so too Beit Yosef in the name of Orach Chayyim, and Rashi explained that in the morning the song would hold up the offering, and in the evening it would not hold it up; but one should not explain that they would not say the song in the Temple except in the morning — if so, there is a difficulty, for is it not a complete mishnah on Rosh Hashana that the Levites became confused in the song, and that means the song of Maariv? So it appears to resolve it. And see Sefer Be'er Sheva, who resolves this difficulty in a different matter far from the view and it is strained. And see in responsa of R' Moshe Alshich.`,

  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt#baer-heitev#1#א": `(א) And Uva Letziyon. When they announce something, one must announce before the chazzan begins Ashrei and not between Ashrei and Lamnatzeiach, and all the more so at the time when they say Ashrei, so that their intention not be nullified — Semag in Choshen Mishpat siman 267 [printed 262]. If an individual comes to the synagogue at the time when they say the order of kedushah, he should say it with them even before his prayer, for there are those who say that an individual does not say it; and likewise he may say it before he says the second verses, namely Uva Letziyon etc. and "And I, this is My covenant" etc., and afterward he says the second verses. And all the more so that he may say Ashrei and Lamnatzeiach afterward in order to say it with the congregation. Magen Avraham.`,

  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt#baer-heitev#1#ב": `(ב) Two. Since anything that is in the language of the Targum may not be said in the plural, and the sign for this is "two read and one translate." Zohar.`,

  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt#baer-heitev#2#א": `(א) Aleinu leshabeach. In his writings he wrote that they should say it after every prayer of the three prayers, and so too in Knesset HaGedolah siman 234; but in Sefer Shetei Yadot in the name of Toldot Yaakov he wrote that one does not say it at Minchah, and at the end of Aleinu one should say the verse "And Hashem shall be king" etc. One should say "and the seat of His glory." One may explain: He is our God, there is no other — Rashbatz. And one may explain: Aleinu with awe and fear, for the entire host of heaven hears and Hashem stands with the heavenly retinue and they all respond and say "Happy is the people that is thus, happy is the people whose God is Hashem" — Magen Avraham. The Arizal would say, and therefore we hope to You with a vav, and so he wrote in Sefer Kanfei Renanim to Mahar"a Azulai, Yad Aharon.`,

  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt#baer-heitev#2#ב": `(ב) And we bow. "And he shall bow and prostrate" — a great prostration. The Arizal — Magen Avraham.`,

  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt#baer-heitev#2#ג": `(ג) Orphan's kaddish. One who is in doubt whether his father or mother died may say kaddish when there is no other mourner there — Maharil. Sons of sons may say kaddish, but the other mourners say two kaddishim, and they are kaddish 1; and there is no difference whether they are from the father's side or the mother's side. And in responsa of Knesset Yechezkel he wrote that specifically a son's son, but a daughter's son does not say kaddish, and all the more so a daughter has no kaddish at all in the synagogue; and if they wish to make a minyan for her, permission is in their hands — see there end of part 14. A yahrzeit has all the kaddishim of that day, even the kaddish of Perakim and of Ruth and of Shir HaShirim — Rambam siman 80, and so too Magen Avraham. And in Knesset Yechezkel there he wrote that everything obligatory, such as prayer kaddish and the kaddish of Shir HaYichud, and likewise Barchi Nafshi and Perakim, belongs to the yahrzeit. But Megillat Kohelet, Ruth, and Shir HaShirim they cast lots, since they are only a mere custom; they are similar to the kaddish of Tehillim and the song of the day, and they cast lots. And the kaddish that they say on Friday night after the psalm "A song for the Shabbat day" is for mourners, unless the yahrzeit is in the company of receiving Shabbat, and everything is according to custom. And in Knesset Yechezkel there he wrote that a yahrzeit that fell on Friday has no connection at all; only mourners with a yahrzeit that falls on Shabbat cast lots — see there. When they cast lots among four or five and among them are two who have equal letters, such as tav, and the others have a smaller count than that, then the lot is void and they all need to cast lots anew, and the two cannot say "the others have already lost their share, we both cast lots." But if one has tav and the others are equal and have less, then certainly the first one won. If two or three cast lots and the next day another one came, the division is void and they need to cast lots anew, and the one who said kaddish at Maariv is not in the lot; and if he was in the city and did not come, he lost his right, for since he did not come he certainly waived to them that he be last. If one said kaddish and the next day another came and said "I say today corresponding to the kaddish you said yesterday, and on the third kaddish we will cast lots," and that one says "since you did not come yesterday I won the kaddish, and the two of today we will divide between us" — the law is with the second, and he says the first kaddish and on the second they cast lots. However, if there are four yahrzeits and three came yesterday, the three say all the prayer kaddishim and the fourth says the kaddish of the song of the day. And if there are many yahrzeits, even though they say all the kaddishim, there is not enough to divide among all; the one who did not come at Maariv lost his right. All the poskim agree that even though one can pray and the other cannot, regarding kaddish the one who prayed did not lose; and if both can pray, they cast lots who is first and who is second. Two who have a yahrzeit and one wishes to leave early on the road tomorrow and said "give me now the kaddish and you take tomorrow," and the second says "no, rather let us cast lots" — the law is with the second, for he can say "I wish to advance myself to the mitzvah." Two compelled ones: one was killed and one died — the son of the killed one takes precedence over the son of the one who died naturally, for since he was killed he has some atonement and he certainly repented before his death, which is not so with one who died on his own. When there is a son of thirty days, they give him all the kaddishim, and the kaddish of the psalm for mourners. In places where the custom is that a resident takes precedence over a guest, nevertheless once there is permission for a guest to pray and say kaddish; and Shach wrote that if there is a son of thirty days, a guest has no kaddish at all. One who learns in a yeshiva has the law of a resident. And a teacher, if he has no wife in another place, is called a resident. And see in Knesset Yechezkel what he wrote on this. And regarding kaddish we do not say "part of a day is like the shell day." And even though one person's relative died in the middle of the week and another's died on Sunday, both are equal on Shabbat. One who mourned his father and afterward his mother died has all the kaddishim by the law of thirty days, and nevertheless he divides the other kaddishim on account of his father with the other mourners — Magen Avraham in the name of Maharan, and Knesset Yechezkel challenged him that if so, one who mourned his father and mother would say kaddish double relative to other mourners, and this is not so, for one remembrance applies here and there; and he resolves it, for example if he is a son of thirty days, then the prayer kaddishim are his and the sons of twelve months say Tehillim kaddish and the like; and if it happened on some day that there are several yahrzeits, then the son of thirty is completely deferred from prayer kaddishim — in this one may say that he divides with the mourners in the other kaddishim on account of his father, but otherwise he does not say kaddish double relative to other mourners, for one remembrance applies here and there. A great son of seven who goes on Shabbat to the synagogue takes precedence over a yahrzeit, and all the more so over a son of thirty; but a small son of seven has the law of thirty and a yahrzeit has one kaddish — Mishna Berurah siman 73, and Shach siman 376 wrote that even for a son of thirty there is one kaddish against a small son of seven, and Magen Avraham ruled that the small one takes precedence over the son of thirty completely, only for a yahrzeit there is one kaddish. One whose mourning was nullified on a festival, or who buried his dead on a festival, has the law of seven and the law of a small seven, since he goes all seven to the synagogue, and a yahrzeit has one kaddish. And if on Shabbat there is a small seven, a great seven, and a yahrzeit, the great one says two kaddishim and the small one one kaddish, so that the great one may say to the small one "I am not taking the third kaddish from you, but from the yahrzeit." In Orach Chayyim there is a doubt whether a great son of seven who makes a minyan in his house perhaps equals on Shabbat a small son of seven and a yahrzeit has one kaddish; and we do not divide on this and we practice as written. A son of thirty has all the prayer kaddishim, and the other mourners of twelve months say Tehillim kaddishim and the like. A yahrzeit resident and a son of thirty resident — a yahrzeit has one kaddish at Maariv, and a son of thirty two kaddishim at Shacharit. A yahrzeit guest and a son of thirty or a son of twelve months resident — a yahrzeit guest has one kaddish. A guest son of thirty and a twelve-month resident divide all the prayer kaddishim. A yahrzeit resident and a yahrzeit guest — the resident takes precedence over the guest. A yahrzeit resident, a yahrzeit guest, and a son of thirty resident — then the guest says one kaddish at Shacharit from the two kaddishim that belong to the son of thirty, and the yahrzeit resident cannot say to the yahrzeit guest "I am first against you," for the guest says "I am not taking from you, but from the son of thirty." If a yahrzeit resident and a yahrzeit guest regarding Tehillim kaddish for which they cast lots — the yahrzeit with the mourners: if the yahrzeit is a resident he waives the lot so that the yahrzeit guest says one kaddish; the mourners may not push back, for against them a yahrzeit guest comes first. However, if they cast lots and the mourners won, the yahrzeit guest says "my luck caused it"; but a yahrzeit guest is with them in the lot, for against them he is as one of them. And in the spreading of Shema before the door, namely the minyan that they make in the ezrah before the door, guest and resident are equal; however, the kaddish of Tehillim and the song of the day and other psalms, and the kaddish after Shir HaShirim and Ruth and Kohelet — a guest has no share, only a yahrzeit, as I wrote. The common custom on the day when they stop saying kaddish at the completion of eleven months — he says all the kaddishim; however, he does not take precedence over a yahrzeit and a son of thirty, and he stops saying kaddish a day or before and not on the same day as the yahrzeit. For example, if one died on the 3rd of Shevat, he must stop on the 2nd of Tevet, for the reason they stop is so that one not think his father wicked; and if he says on the 3rd of Tevet it will be the first day of the twelfth month and it will be considered a month, as if he said twelve months — therefore he says on the 2nd of Tevet and stops on that day, and the 3rd of Shevat is the yahrzeit. A yahrzeit is always the day of death and not the day of burial, even if the burial was delayed many days, even in the first year. If his mother died and the father objects that he not say kaddish, he has no power to protest. A guest who said one kaddish in some synagogue has no further kaddish in any synagogue of this community until the thirtieth day. All this I brought from the words of early and late poskim; and see in Knesset Yechezkel end of part 14, and see in Sefer Eliyah Rabbah, who brought several laws and responsa on these matters.`,

  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt#baer-heitev#2#ד": `(ד) Evening and morning after the prayer. Taz challenged, for it is stated in the Gemara: you have nothing that precedes the morning tamid except the incense alone, and they instituted prayer corresponding to the tamidim — if so, we should precede pitum haketoret before the prayer of eighteen. See there what he raised, and according to this I practiced myself to say pitum haketoret before Baruch She'amar until here. And Magen Avraham challenged: why do we say it after Musaf, and behold the incense preceded the musafim for everyone? And he resolves that perhaps our intention is to take leave from words of Torah — see there. And in Shelah he wrote to say it at Shacharit before the prayer and after the prayer. And in the evening one says before Minchah prayer, but not after Maariv prayer, for burning incense is invalid after the lighting of candles — see Knesset HaGedolah and Magen Avraham and in Sefer Be'er Sheva.`,

  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt#baer-heitev#2#ה": `(ה) The song that the Levites. I found written that what Magen Avraham wrote from the mishnah "the song that the Levites" etc. is an error; for the Tur's intention is to say each day the song belonging to that day, and so too Rambam in the order of prayer and so too Knesset HaGedolah; and Magen Avraham wrote, it appears to me that the Tur's intention is to say the mishnah as well in order to take leave from words of Torah — see there — and he concludes that on Shabbat the custom is to say the mishnah as printed in all siddurim. Knesset HaGedolah wrote: I saw a custom in all places on Rosh Chodesh and the intermediate days of the festival and Chanukah and Purim — in place of the song that the Levites would say, they say another song related to the day: on Rosh Chodesh Barchi Nafshi; on the intermediate days of Pesach Hodu LaHashem; on the intermediate days of the festival "As a hart panteth"; on Chanukah the psalm "A song of the dedication of the House to David"; and on Purim "Upon the hind of the dawn." And I wonder who permitted them to change the song that the Levites would say every day — see there what he raised, that one should not skip the song the Levites would say every day, but rather that they say the song the Levites would say every day and the song related to the day, and so too the Rama siman 25. However, some give precedence there to the song of each day first, for frequent precedes infrequent; but on Chanukah one says the psalm of the dedication of the House before the psalm that the Levites would say every day because of publicizing the miracle — see there, and see Magen Avraham.`,

  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt#baer-heitev#2#ו": `(ו) Perhaps he will skip. See what Rabbi Be'er Sheva challenged in tractate Kerisos daf 46 on Beit Yosef this siman, and so too in Taz and Magen Avraham. And in responsa Sha'ar Efrayim siman 16 he wishes to resolve Beit Yosef — see there — and it is not so the implication of Beit Yosef's words, for from Beit Yosef it implies that even if one did not put in it a herb that produces smoke for which one is not liable to death — see there — and this is the opposite of the words of the Rabbi author of Sha'ar Efrayim, and it requires further study; and see in Sefer Mishneh LaMelech, and in Sefer Mikra'ei Kodesh daf 125 side b, and in Sefer Birchas HaZevach, and in Peri Chadash (and see in Sefer Eliyah Rabbah, who expanded greatly on this). "Water of feet" mentioned in pitum haketoret — Kolbo wrote that there is one spring whose name is mei raglayim. And what Magen Avraham wrote "because of honor" means that that spring has a disgraceful name; and in Be'er Sheva he expanded to prove that he literally means mei raglayim — see there. And see in Bnei Yaakov.`,

  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt#baer-heitev#2#ז": `(ז) And he prostrates himself and goes out. Maharil made three prostrations when he went from his place toward the Holy Ark, and when going out from the door of the synagogue each time like a student taking leave of his teacher; when he goes out from the synagogue he should not go out with his back to the sanctuary but should turn aside, and likewise when descending from the bimah — see there Magen Avraham.`,

  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt#beer-hagolah#1#א": `Sotah 49`,

  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt#beer-hagolah#1#ב": `Tur`,

  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt#beer-hagolah#2#_": `Rav Amram Gaon`,

  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt#beur-hagra#1#א": `(א) 61 they translate etc. See Rashi there s.v. akdushah etc., and so too in Zohar parashat Terumah — see there.`,

  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt#beur-hagra#1#ב": `(ב) And one must etc. For the world stands upon it, as written in the Gemara there.`,

  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt#beur-hagra#1#ג": `(ג) And in kedushah etc. Zohar there.`,

  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt#beur-hagra#2#א": `(א) 62 forbidden etc. For the above reason.`,

  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt#beur-hagra#2#ב": `(ב) And they say etc. In Tana deVei Eliyahu: one who says Yitgadel redeems his father from punishments, and the well-known story in the aggadah.`,

  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt#beur-hagra#2#ג": `(ג) And one may say pitum etc. For it was offered in the morning and evening, as written in the Torah and in siman 1, 65.`,

  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt#beur-hagra#2#ד": `(ד) And they say etc. See Magen Avraham.`,

  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt#beur-hagra#2#ה": `(ה) And we say etc. In chapter 4 of Yoma and chapter 1 of Kerisos.`,

  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt#beur-hagra#2#ו": `(ו) And he prostrates himself and goes out. As written in Tamid at the end of his service: and he prostrated himself and went out.`,

  "chatam-sofer/part-001.txt#chatam-sofer#1#_": `In Magen Avraham note 2 — end of his words. And I saw in Noda B'Yehuda, and it is explained in Levush and Minhagim, that prayer kaddishim specifically belong to yahrzeit, namely two on weekdays — Maariv and Shacharit — and three on Shabbat — Maariv, Musaf, and Minchah; and if there is at Shacharit Shir HaShirim or Ruth, that belongs to him as well. And what Magen Avraham wrote of Perakim — Knesset Yechezkel added also of Barchi Nafshi; nevertheless this does not include the song of the day nor any psalm kaddish, for otherwise you would find three on weekdays and four on Shabbat, for the psalm of the day is by law to say every day and presumably they say kaddish after it, as Magen Avraham wrote in this siman note 4. And what is the reason he wrote two on weekdays? Rather perforce this is not prayer kaddish. And in responsa of Knesset Yechezkel he wrote that the song of glory kaddish belongs to yahrzeit, and one must say that for this reason he did not account for three on weekdays and four on Shabbat, because they did not practice saying the song of glory in all places; nevertheless I do not know from where he has this, and so I found written in the margin that he wondered at him on this; nevertheless one does not deviate from the custom.`,

  "chatam-sofer/part-001.txt#chatam-sofer#2#_": `(There) in Orach Chayyim therefore one buys kaddish. Noda B'Yehuda: it appears to me there is proof from Yalkut Yeshayahu siman 29 — "Yaakov redeemed Avraham" — and see what Tosafos wrote in tractate Sanhedrin daf 9 side b s.v. shefadu — see there — and in waiving his honor from the author of Knesset Yechezkel, which was hidden from him.`,

  "eshel-avraham/part-001.txt#eshel-avraham#2#_": `Orphan. Regarding that minors who have not reached education say kaddish and the congregation answers after them; and likewise what they say after learning mishnah, beraita of Rosh Chodesh — Hashem wished to give merit to the Land of Israel etc. — see Tosafos in Yoreh De'ah siman 52 and siman 31. And customs of kaddish see in Ba'er Heitev note 5. A group that studies every day with ten — their custom is that if one of them has a yahrzeit or mourning, heaven forbid, he says all the kaddishim; and since there is no mourner among them they hired all the kaddishim to another who is not from the members of the group, and they did not stipulate that if, heaven forbid, mourning befell one of them the hire would be cancelled; and afterward mourning befell one of them — Eliyah Rabbah raised in responsa of Shev Yaakov question 7 that they should divide the kaddishim equally.`,

  "levushei-serad/part-001.txt#levushei-serad#1#_": `In Shulchan Aruch seif 2 — the order is thus called in the Gemara "kedushah and Uva Letziyon."`,

  "levushei-serad/part-001.txt#levushei-serad#2#_": `Magen Avraham note 2, and see siman 113. Even though we say there that one may not bow and prostrate except in a place that the Sages instituted, nevertheless when we bow it is permitted, and so too there Taz note 4 — see there his reason.`,

  "machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt#machatzit-hashekel#1#א": `Magen Avraham wrote: when they announce etc., and Semag in Choshen Mishpat siman 267 note 4 — so it should read.`,

  "machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt#machatzit-hashekel#1#ב": `This is his language there: even though the order of kedushah is a matter in itself, like Maariv to Minchah, nevertheless immediately after the kaddish of Tachanun the order of kedushah begins, and therefore they repeat and say Ashrei; therefore it is better to announce before Ashrei, and better after the conclusion of the prayer — end of his words.`,

  "machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt#machatzit-hashekel#1#ג": `And so too in Beit Yosef siman 559, who wrote that in places where they announce a public fast on the Shabbat before it, they announce after the haftarah before Ashrei — behold, since Ashrei belongs to Musaf, one may not interrupt between it and the Musaf prayer; the same law applies here, since Ashrei belongs to the kedushah that was instituted for its sake, one may not interrupt between Ashrei and kedushah.`,

  "machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt#machatzit-hashekel#1#ד": `For there are those who say that an individual etc. — meaning the order of kedushah, as above siman 59; and it is that we hold that even an individual says it, as Rama wrote siman 59; nevertheless, wherever possible to go according to everyone, it is preferable.`,

  "machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt#machatzit-hashekel#1#ה": `And the same law applies that one may say before he says the second verses, for apparently the two verses have no connection to kedushah; and see in Beit Yosef the reason for saying them. And even though Levush wrote that for the reason that Rabbi Beit Yosef wrote in the name of Kolbo — why do we say this kedushah — there is connection of the two aforementioned verses to kedushah, and this is his language in Kolbo: because they could not say kedushah within the prayer, for they decreed that one not answer kedushah, and enemies were lying in wait for them if they answered kedushah; and after the prayer, when the enemies were going away, they would call and "this called to that" etc., namely the triple kedushah, and they translate it because of the word. The same applies twice corresponding to the kedushah of Yotzer in prayer; and even though now the decree is nullified, nevertheless the kedushah of the order was not nullified, for they fear lest sin cause etc. — end of his words. And so too in Levush: therefore they then preceded saying Uva Letziyon Go'el, for we apologize before Hashem for having delayed kedushah until now because subjugation to the kingdom causes us to be prevented from saying it within the prayer; therefore we pray for redemption and say Uva Letziyon etc.; and likewise they prevent us from engaging in Torah, therefore they also say the verse "And I, this is My covenant" etc. to remind before Him, may He be blessed, that He promised us the Torah would not depart from us; and afterward "He sits — the praises of Israel" — they expounded from this that Hashem rests His Shechinah among us to hear the praises of Israel; and "sits" is language of dwelling, and we say this in language of supplication even though we finished it, for it is not in rebellion and treachery, therefore He forgives that He delay to hear our prayer as is His way, may He be blessed; and if so, properly there is connection of the two verses to kedushah; nevertheless this is not the essence of saying kedushah, but according to what is written in tractate Sotah daf 49: from the day the Temple was destroyed there is no day whose curse is not greater than its fellow's; and the world — on what does it stand? Since the curse goes and increases, we say the kedushah of the order and Amen Yehei Shemei Rabbah of the aggadah; and Rashi explained: the kedushah of the order was instituted because all Israel engage in Torah every day a small matter that they say — the verse and its translation (for then they understood the translation), and behold it is as if engaging in Torah; and since it is practiced among all Israel etc., and here there are two sanctifications of the Name and Torah study is beloved — end of his words. And for this reason there is no continuation of the two verses of Uva Letziyon etc. to kedushah; and according to this, properly one should precede saying kedushah with the congregation.`,

  "machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt#machatzit-hashekel#1#ו": `And see in Yalkut parashat Ekev remez 955 at the end, where he gave a reason for the kedushah of the order; and it is also a kind of reason of the Gemara in Sotah mentioned above.`,

  "kaf-hachayyim/part-001.txt#kaf-hachayyim#1#_": `(1) [Seif 1] They translate kedushah and Uva Letziyon, and when they announce something one must announce before the chazzan begins Ashrei and not between Ashrei and Lamnatzeiach, and all the more so at the time when they say Ashrei so that their intention not be nullified — six pillars and Semag in Choshen Mishpat siman 267, and so too in Beit Yosef siman 559, Magen Avraham at the beginning of the siman end of seif 2 letter 1, Chasam Sofer letter 8, Kaf HaChayyim siman 109 letter 2; and Orach Chayyim Acharon wrote that what they go around for a donation and the like is not proper, and all the more so at the time of repetition of the prayer, for they confuse the intention — end of his words. However, Tosafos Chayyim letter 1 wrote to resolve the custom that it is not similar to an announcement that announces news, and thereby intention is nullified; not so with something practiced always, whose intention is not nullified thereby.`,

  "kaf-hachayyim/part-001.txt#kaf-hachayyim#2#_": `(2) There they translate kedushah and Uva Letziyon and one says Uva Letziyon and "And I, this is My covenant," and afterward "And You are holy" — Tur in this siman 131 in the name of Rambam and Rav Amram, may he rest in Eden; and see above siman 131 seif 1 in the gloss, who wrote that one must say before Uva Letziyon Ashrei and Lamnatzeiach, and so too in Sha'ar HaKavanot daf 49 side b, and see there the reason in secret; and he wrote further there in Sha'ar HaKavanot that one must say three times Ashrei before Tehillah LeDor, and they are "Happy are those who dwell in Your house" etc., "Happy is the people that is thus," "Happy is the people" etc., and see there the reason in secret.`,
};

